# keur quality control — a printable Unicode glyph written as a numeric \uXXXX
# escape instead of the literal UTF-8 character.
#
# Why this exists: SonarCloud (cpp:S6659) flagged keur's TUI glyphs (✓ ☐ • ◦ ▼ ▶
# and the → arrow) written as \u2713-style escapes. The braced forms Sonar
# suggests (\u{2713}) are C++23; keur builds -std=c++20 on gcc/clang/MSVC, so the
# portable, reviewable fix is to write the literal UTF-8 character in the source
# string. That also reads better in a diff. This rule makes it a keur-native
# check so the next escaped glyph is caught at commit time — fix + control.
#
# What it flags — a \uXXXX escape for a codepoint in the printable/symbol range
# (>= U+00A1), i.e. a real glyph. It deliberately does NOT flag control
# characters (ESC \033 / \x1b for ANSI SGR sequences), which have no readable
# literal and are legitimately written as escapes.
#
# WARN, not block: a readability/portability smell, not a bug. Tests excluded —
# key-decoding tests assert on literal escape sequences on purpose.
#
# @see SonarCloud cpp:S6659.
id: KEUR-QUAL-011
title: Write printable Unicode glyphs as literal UTF-8, not \uXXXX escapes
category: quality
severity: warning
engine: pattern
action: warn
skip_comments: true
target:
  extensions: .c .cpp .cc .cxx .h .hpp .hh
  exclude_paths: test/ tests/ _test.cpp vendor/ build/ dist/
patterns:
  # Any \uXXXX escape (4 hex). keur's TUI uses only printable glyphs
  # (bullets, arrows, checkmarks, middle dot); there are no \u00xx control
  # escapes here. ANSI ESC is written \033 (octal), so it never matches \u.
  - regex: \\u[0-9A-Fa-f]{4}
    message: "Printable Unicode glyph written as a \\uXXXX escape — write the literal UTF-8 character instead (Sonar suggests \\u{...}, but that is C++23; UTF-8 is portable on -std=c++20 and clearer in diffs). @see KEUR-QUAL-011, SonarCloud cpp:S6659"
confidence: 0.6
fix: "Replace the \\uXXXX escape with the literal UTF-8 glyph in the string (e.g. \\u2713 -> the ✓ character). Keep numeric escapes only for control characters like ESC (\\033) in ANSI sequences, which have no readable literal."
